WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.800 --> 00:00:11.139
RICHARD PRUM: People have been interested in the colors of dinosaurs
for as long as they've known that the fossils existed.

00:00:11.139 --> 00:00:14.999
They interpreted the colors of dinosaurs based on living reptiles--

00:00:14.999 --> 00:00:16.169
scaly 

00:00:16.169 --> 00:00:17.660
and usually

00:00:17.660 --> 00:00:24.659
dark browns or muted greens. In the
late nineties, people began to discover

00:00:24.659 --> 00:00:27.249
feathered dinosaurs-- dinos with dino-fuzz.

00:00:27.249 --> 00:00:29.740
At that point it became interesting to know

00:00:29.740 --> 00:00:36.740
what that-- how those feathers could have influenced
the appearance of them.

00:00:37.530 --> 00:00:38.910
JAKOB VINTHER: How we found out is that--

00:00:38.910 --> 00:00:41.720
fossil feathers, they preserve the melanin--

00:00:41.720 --> 00:00:44.400
that is the pigment that gives the color to the feather.

00:00:44.400 --> 00:00:48.890
So the way that that I got the idea that we
could potentially use this to put colors on dinosaurs

00:00:48.890 --> 00:00:50.650
came from a very odd angle,

00:00:50.650 --> 00:00:53.020
'cause I was actually looking at fossil squids.

00:00:53.020 --> 00:00:55.610
And we find fossil squids 

00:00:55.610 --> 00:00:57.830
that have the ink cells preserved,

00:00:57.830 --> 00:01:00.740
and quite interestingly, when you look at this
fossilized

00:01:00.740 --> 00:01:01.870
ink, you can actually see that

00:01:01.870 --> 00:01:03.980
it preserves the pigments.

00:01:03.980 --> 00:01:05.290
And that made me think that

00:01:05.290 --> 00:01:06.590
we should be able to find

00:01:07.590 --> 00:01:11.229
this pigment, which is melanin, in other
kinds of fossils,

00:01:11.229 --> 00:01:17.700
such as, for example, feathers.

00:01:17.700 --> 00:01:20.720
PRUM: Melanin is created by pigment cells

00:01:20.720 --> 00:01:25.070
which package them in little organelles called
melanosomes. These melanosomes are

00:01:25.070 --> 00:01:30.350
tiny little packages, they come
in oblong or rounded

00:01:30.350 --> 00:01:35.500
shapes.
VINTHER: We have a locality in Denmark that is about fifty five million years old

00:01:35.500 --> 00:01:36.780
where we find fossilized birds

00:01:36.780 --> 00:01:40.800
with feathers. So when I took this fossil under a scanning electro-microscope,

00:01:40.800 --> 00:01:44.190
and started zooming in on the fossil feathers, 

00:01:44.190 --> 00:01:49.240
I was just met by this ocean of little
sausage-shaped structures,

00:01:49.240 --> 00:01:51.320
and that confirmed my expectations that melanin

00:01:51.320 --> 00:01:53.110
is very abundant

00:01:53.110 --> 00:01:55.500
in fossils, and also 

00:01:55.500 --> 00:01:56.650
it means that we can

00:01:56.650 --> 00:02:03.650
use this sort of information to put colors on dinosaurs.

00:02:04.950 --> 00:02:06.330
PRUM: What we were able to do

00:02:06.330 --> 00:02:08.119
was compare the shape

00:02:08.119 --> 00:02:09.979
and size and density 

00:02:09.979 --> 00:02:14.169
of the melanosomes in these fossil feathers
to the feathers of living birds,

00:02:14.169 --> 00:02:15.970
and thereby

00:02:15.970 --> 00:02:17.769
interpret or predict the color

00:02:17.769 --> 00:02:21.839
of these fossil feathers when the animal
was alive.

00:02:21.839 --> 00:02:23.930
This has allowed us to create the first

00:02:23.930 --> 00:02:25.509
description

00:02:25.509 --> 00:02:27.209
of the plumage color

00:02:27.209 --> 00:02:34.209
of an extinct dinosaur in history.

00:02:35.799 --> 00:02:39.260
DEREK BRIGGS: The dinosaur is an animal called Anchiornis

00:02:39.260 --> 00:02:41.209
and it's about the size of a chicken.

00:02:41.209 --> 00:02:44.189
And it has a rather spectacular plumage, including

00:02:44.189 --> 00:02:45.329
a red color on

00:02:45.329 --> 00:02:46.579
the crest.

00:02:46.579 --> 00:02:47.499
And

00:02:47.499 --> 00:02:49.469
spotting and bracketing

00:02:49.469 --> 00:02:53.159
of black and white and grey colors on the rest of the plumage.

00:02:53.159 --> 00:02:57.329
VINTHER: What Ricky said when he first saw this
thing and we started realizing what kind of

00:02:57.329 --> 00:03:00.529
color patterns it had, he said, it looks like a
spangled hen bird.

00:03:00.529 --> 00:03:03.519
If you see this Anchiornis, as it's called,

00:03:04.310 --> 00:03:08.109
and just have a quick look at it, and look away, I think most people would say that it

00:03:08.109 --> 00:03:15.099
looks like chicken.

00:03:15.099 --> 00:03:16.439
BRIGGS: This fossil is from

00:03:16.439 --> 00:03:20.799
the latest Jurassic of Liaoning province in China.

00:03:20.799 --> 00:03:25.179
So these Chinese examples are extraordinary
in that many of them do have an outline of

00:03:25.179 --> 00:03:30.549
the feathers of most extinct birds and these
early dinosaurs.

00:03:30.549 --> 00:03:35.019
VINTHER: So the interesting prospect of this is that
we can see that melanin is

00:03:35.019 --> 00:03:37.489
in all these fossil feathers.

00:03:37.489 --> 00:03:44.489
It's the rule rather than the exception.

00:03:44.930 --> 00:03:46.050
PRUM: The future

00:03:46.050 --> 00:03:49.669
in dinosaur paleontology and the paleontology of birds 

00:03:49.669 --> 00:03:50.430
and mammals

00:03:50.430 --> 00:03:51.700
will be that we

00:03:51.700 --> 00:03:54.729
we will probably be able to reconstruct in
some

00:03:54.729 --> 00:03:55.299
detail

00:03:55.299 --> 00:03:56.480
the color 

00:03:56.480 --> 00:03:57.999
that they had in life.

00:03:57.999 --> 00:03:59.309
This is like writing

00:03:59.309 --> 00:04:01.590
the first field guide page in

00:04:01.590 --> 00:04:03.280
the field guide to the dinosaurs.

00:04:03.280 --> 00:04:04.349
And it's one of the most exciting

00:04:04.349 --> 00:04:05.670
moments of science I've ever had. 

